{
  "scenario_id": "sc-3c83fd",
  "overall_assessment": "anomalies",
  "findings": [
    {
      "benefit": "CI",
      "years": [2017, 2019],
      "pattern": "shock",
      "direction": "decrease",
      "magnitude": "CI A/E declines from ~1.10 (2016) to a 0.73 trough (2019) — the scored 2018-19 excursion is -0.29 (score 6.7), i.e. claims running ~20-30% below expected — then steps up ~+0.16 in 2020 to a flat ~1.06-1.10 plateau (claims ~6-10% above expected) that holds through 2024; the end level sits +0.06 above the line's baseline and does not revert to it.",
      "confidence": 0.85,
      "evidence": [
        "ae_ci_by_year.csv: 2015=1.051, 2016=1.098, 2017=0.927, 2018=0.808, 2019=0.731, 2020=1.067, 2021=1.100, 2022=1.084, 2023=1.090, 2024=1.055",
        "Computed pack CI: 2018-19 excursion -0.290 (score 6.7, the dominant level deviation); +0.156 step at 2020; onset ratio 1.16 flagged as 'a one-off level change, not a build-up' (i.e. shock, not drift); end level +0.058 vs own baseline (clean lines stay within 0.008) so the post-2020 elevation is persistent",
        "CI YoY sd x4.73 is mild overdispersion (normal texture, z-scores indicative only — not itself an anomaly); CROSS-LINE shows CI as one of 3 lines taking a large 2017 swing (book-wide one-off, read in level rows, not a dispersion finding) — the level-row consequence is material only for CI: Death's 2016 spike is z=2.8 and reverts in 2017 (below the ~4.5 threshold), and IP's 2017 step is only -0.028 (A/E stays within 0.98-1.00, overall 1.0023)"
      ],
      "recommended_action": "Investigate the 2017-2019 CI under-claiming and the persistent 2020-2024 elevation: test for a claim reporting/booking lag (a dip followed by an overshoot is the classic signature of late-booked claims), data-quality or claim-definition changes introduced around 2017, and product-mix / underwriting / incidence shifts from 2020. Because the post-2020 level runs ~6-10% above expected and does not revert (end level +0.06 vs baseline), review CI pricing and valuation assumptions and consider a level or lag adjustment in the experience study. Death, TPD, and IP (including all IP termination/recovery rates by diagnosis) are otherwise consistent with noise and need no follow-up."
    }
  ]
}

